<Project>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- Standalone consumers do not inherit this repo's Directory.Build.props; default from the env var. -->
    <VintageStoryPath Condition="'$(VintageStoryPath)' == ''">$(VINTAGE_STORY)</VintageStoryPath>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    Shared fix for E2E consumers (test projects, samples) that both reference VintagestoryAPI
    directly and pull in dependencies (test SDKs, etc.) that transitively bring their own
    Newtonsoft.Json. That transitive copy (commonly 13.0.1, a plain NuGet release) lands in the
    consumer's own output directory and shadows the game's own copy - Newtonsoft.Json 13.0.4.30916,
    a Vintage Story-shipped build that is not a public NuGet release - because default assembly
    probing against the output directory wins over Atlas's AssemblyResolve fallback, which only
    runs once probing has already failed. VintagestoryLib/VintagestoryAPI are compiled against the
    exact game copy, so a mismatched version causes a MissingMethodException at runtime (e.g. inside
    ServerConfig.Save()) instead of a clear error. Import this file from any project that needs a
    real Vintage Story install for E2E/integration testing:

      <Import Project="..\..\build\Atlas.E2E.targets" />

    VintageStoryPath defaults from the VINTAGE_STORY environment variable for standalone consumers
    that do not inherit this repo's Directory.Build.props.
  -->

  <Target Name="CopyGameNewtonsoftJson" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(VintageStoryPath)' != '' AND Exists('$(VintageStoryPath)\Lib\Newtonsoft.Json.dll')">
    <Copy SourceFiles="$(VintageStoryPath)\Lib\Newtonsoft.Json.dll"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="false" />
  </Target>

  <Target Name="WarnMissingGameNewtonsoftJson" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(VintageStoryPath)' == '' OR !Exists('$(VintageStoryPath)\Lib\Newtonsoft.Json.dll')">
    <Warning Text="VintageStoryPath is unset or '$(VintageStoryPath)\Lib\Newtonsoft.Json.dll' does not exist: the game's Newtonsoft.Json will not be copied over the transitive NuGet copy, and E2E runs will likely fail at runtime with a MissingMethodException from Newtonsoft.Json instead of a clear setup error. Set the VINTAGE_STORY environment variable to a valid install." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    A VintagestoryAPI.dll copy in the test output wins default assembly probing over the game
    install's copy, and the game's LoggerBase static constructor reads the throw site's source
    file name from pdb debug info: without VintagestoryAPI.pdb next to the loaded dll, every
    scenario dies at server boot in an opaque TypeInitializationException (NullReferenceException
    in LoggerBase..cctor). Atlas also fails fast at runtime with a clear AtlasSetupException; this
    warning surfaces the misconfiguration already at build time. It typically means the dll was
    vendored (copied into the repo without its pdb) or copied by a custom build step - a plain
    <Reference> with a HintPath into the game install copies the pdb automatically.
  -->
  <Target Name="WarnMissingVintagestoryApiPdb" AfterTargets="Build"
          Condition="Exists('$(TargetDir)VintagestoryAPI.dll') AND !Exists('$(TargetDir)VintagestoryAPI.pdb')">
    <Warning Text="VintagestoryAPI.dll is in the output directory ('$(TargetDir)') without VintagestoryAPI.pdb. The game's LoggerBase derives source paths from pdb debug info in its static constructor, so every Atlas scenario will fail at server boot with a TypeInitializationException (NullReferenceException in LoggerBase..cctor). Ship the matching VintagestoryAPI.pdb next to the dll, or stop copying the dll (Private=false on the reference) so the game install's own copy is loaded." />
  </Target>

  <!--
    MSBuild staging sugar: a test project can reference its mod-under-test as an ordinary
    ProjectReference, tagged with <AtlasMod>true</AtlasMod>, instead of hand-writing a relative
    path into [assembly: AtlasMods(...)]:

      <ProjectReference Include="..\MyMod\MyMod.csproj">
        <AtlasMod>true</AtlasMod>
      </ProjectReference>

    _ResolvedProjectReferencePaths is the item MSBuild produces after resolving each
    ProjectReference to its actual build output (respecting the referenced project's own
    OutputType/TargetPath); custom metadata placed on the original ProjectReference item (like
    AtlasMod here) flows through onto the resolved item automatically, so no matching back by
    path is needed. This was confirmed empirically: building a throwaway consumer with a
    diagnostic target logging %(_ResolvedProjectReferencePaths.AtlasMod) showed the metadata
    present on the resolved item, unchanged. The item also carries MSBuildSourceProjectFile, the
    referenced project's own file, which is where the project folder below comes from.

    Dll mod or folder mod (issue #22): a tagged reference is a folder mod when a modinfo.json
    sits in its build output or in its project folder, and a dll mod otherwise. Writing the bare
    dll path only works for mods carrying an assembly-level ModInfo attribute. A mod discovered
    through a modinfo.json (no assembly attribute: Manifold's case, and the common csproj layout
    with <None Include="modinfo.json" CopyToOutputDirectory="..." />) is rejected by the game's
    ModLoader when staged as a bare dll: it takes the dll-mod path, never looks at the
    modinfo.json, and reports "no .dll files that contain ... a ModInfo attribute" even though
    the dll has a ModSystem. A dll mod keeps the bare resolved dll path in the manifest, as before.

    A modinfo.json that the mod project generates at build counts like a checked-in one, as long as
    it is in the project's build output when that project has finished building: this target runs
    after ResolveProjectReferences, which builds the referenced project first. A target of the mod
    project that runs after Build and writes the file to $(OutDir) works, and so does one that
    writes it under obj/ before AssignTargetPaths and adds it as a None item with
    CopyToOutputDirectory. A template that is itself called modinfo.json and sits next to the
    project file hides the generated copy (layer 2 below copies the project's file over the build
    output's), so keep the template under another name. Atlas generates no modinfo.json itself.

    Folder mods (issues #171 and #170): the manifest gets one folder per mod,
    $(TargetDir)atlas-mods/<name>/, assembled by this target. <name> is the referenced
    assembly's file name without its extension. ModStager names a staged mod after its file or
    folder, and every mod project builds into a folder called net10.0 (or whatever the
    framework is), so listing the build outputs themselves merged two mods into one folder, which
    the engine then refused as a whole. The assembly name is different for every mod and does not
    change between configurations, which also makes atlas-mods/<name> a fixed path for
    [AtlasWorld(Mods = ...)]. The folder is rebuilt from scratch on every build, so a file
    removed from the project does not linger in it, in two layers:

      1. the referenced project's whole build output, subfolders included (what the sugar
         staged before, as the output folder itself), without the dll, pdb and xml files of the
         other tagged mods: a mod built on another mod holds that mod's dll in its output, the
         other mod is staged as a mod of its own, and two mod dlls in one folder make the engine
         refuse the folder;
      2. the project folder's assets/ folder and modinfo.json, copied over the first layer.

    The project wins on a file both layers hold. A mod that keeps assets/ at the project root
    and does not copy it to its output, or whose output holds a stale copy of a file, is staged
    with the project's files; a file only the build output has (a generated asset) is kept. The
    project folder is read even when the csproj copies assets/ to the output as well: after a
    csproj stops copying them, IncrementalClean can leave empty assets/ folders behind in the
    output, so an assets/ folder there says nothing about what the project holds. Two tagged
    folder mods with one assembly name would share one folder here, which is the merge this
    avoids, so that is an error (ATLAS002).

    Not covered: assets that live outside the project folder (only assets/ and modinfo.json next
    to the project file are read), a mod project that builds into its own project folder, and a
    Windows machine (paths go through NormalizePath and NormalizeDirectory, but only Linux has
    run it). Multi-targeting needs nothing of its own: each target framework pass of the
    consumer resolves the reference to the matching build output and stages under its own
    $(TargetDir); the TargetFramework condition skips the outer dispatch pass. Checked on
    throwaway projects with a mod project building net8.0 and net10.0 and a consumer listing two
    frameworks: each pass staged the output of the framework it resolves to.

    The manifest is written after ResolveProjectReferences (so the resolved paths exist) and
    before Build completes, directly into $(TargetDir) alongside the test assembly, one absolute
    path per line (dll path or mod folder path, per reference, no trailing separator), via
    WriteLinesToFile(Overwrite=true) so re-builds do not append. If no ProjectReference carries
    AtlasMod=true, any stale manifest from a previous build (e.g. after removing the metadata) is
    deleted rather than left behind with outdated content.

    AttributeMapper (Atlas.XUnit) reads this file at test-discovery time, when present, and
    appends its paths after any [assembly: AtlasMods(...)] paths. ModStager.Stage (src/Atlas)
    stages each line as either a folder (copied recursively) or a single dll, depending on
    whether the path is a directory or a file, see its Exists() dispatch.

    Cost note: this file ships buildTransitive, so the target runs on every build of every
    package consumer. With no folder mod it is a cheap no-op (a few item transforms and
    Exists checks, no file glob: the globs sit in an item group that only runs when there is a
    folder mod); that always-run cost is deliberate, it is what keeps the stale-manifest delete
    reliable on incremental builds (no Inputs/Outputs means no up-to-date skip). A tagged
    folder mod pays one copy of its build output and assets/ per build for the same reason.

    The TargetFramework condition skips the outer dispatch pass of a multi-targeting build
    (TargetFrameworks plural), where no per-TFM $(TargetDir) is meaningful; the inner
    per-framework passes each run the target normally.
  -->
  <Target Name="WriteAtlasModManifest" AfterTargets="ResolveProjectReferences" BeforeTargets="Build"
          Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' != ''">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AtlasModRef Include="@(_ResolvedProjectReferencePaths)" Condition="'%(_ResolvedProjectReferencePaths.AtlasMod)' == 'true'">
        <ModOutDir>%(RootDir)%(Directory)</ModOutDir>
        <ModProjectDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory($([System.IO.Path]::GetDirectoryName('%(MSBuildSourceProjectFile)'))))</ModProjectDir>
        <StageDir>$([MSBuild]::NormalizeDirectory('$(TargetDir)', 'atlas-mods', '%(Filename)'))</StageDir>
        <StageFolder>$([MSBuild]::NormalizePath('$(TargetDir)', 'atlas-mods', '%(Filename)'))</StageFolder>
      </_AtlasModRef>
      <_AtlasModFolder Include="@(_AtlasModRef)"
                       Condition="Exists('%(_AtlasModRef.ModOutDir)modinfo.json') OR Exists('%(_AtlasModRef.ModProjectDir)modinfo.json')" />
      <_AtlasModDll Include="@(_AtlasModRef)"
                    Condition="!Exists('%(_AtlasModRef.ModOutDir)modinfo.json') AND !Exists('%(_AtlasModRef.ModProjectDir)modinfo.json')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!-- Batched on the assembly name, so each bucket holds the projects that share one name and the
         message names those and no others. -->
    <Error Condition="'%(_AtlasModFolder.Filename)' != '' AND '@(_AtlasModFolder->Count())' != '1'"
           Code="ATLAS002"
           Text="AtlasMod project references that build an assembly with the same name ('%(_AtlasModFolder.Filename)') would be staged into one folder (atlas-mods/%(_AtlasModFolder.Filename)), merging their mods: @(_AtlasModFolder->'%(MSBuildSourceProjectFile)', ', '). Give each mod project its own AssemblyName." />

    <RemoveDir Directories="@(_AtlasModFolder->'%(StageFolder)')" Condition="'@(_AtlasModFolder)' != ''" />

    <PropertyGroup>
      <_AtlasModNames>;@(_AtlasModRef->'%(Filename)', ';');</_AtlasModNames>
    </PropertyGroup>

    <!-- The group's own condition is what keeps these globs from running without a folder mod: an
         empty batch still runs the includes once, with empty metadata, and "**" then walks the
         consumer's whole project directory. -->
    <ItemGroup Condition="'@(_AtlasModFolder)' != ''">
      <_AtlasModBuildFile Include="%(_AtlasModFolder.ModOutDir)**">
        <StageDir>%(_AtlasModFolder.StageDir)</StageDir>
        <ModName>%(_AtlasModFolder.Filename)</ModName>
      </_AtlasModBuildFile>
      <!-- A mod built on another tagged mod holds that mod's dll in its output (a ProjectReference
           copies it), and the other mod is staged as a mod of its own: two mod dlls in one folder
           make the engine refuse the folder. -->
      <_AtlasModBuildFile Remove="@(_AtlasModBuildFile)"
                          Condition="'%(_AtlasModBuildFile.RecursiveDir)' == ''
                                     AND '%(_AtlasModBuildFile.Filename)' != '%(_AtlasModBuildFile.ModName)'
                                     AND $(_AtlasModNames.Contains(';%(_AtlasModBuildFile.Filename);'))
                                     AND ('%(_AtlasModBuildFile.Extension)' == '.dll' OR '%(_AtlasModBuildFile.Extension)' == '.pdb' OR '%(_AtlasModBuildFile.Extension)' == '.xml')" />
      <_AtlasModProjectFile Include="%(_AtlasModFolder.ModProjectDir)assets/**">
        <StageDir>%(_AtlasModFolder.StageDir)assets/</StageDir>
      </_AtlasModProjectFile>
      <_AtlasModProjectFile Include="%(_AtlasModFolder.ModProjectDir)modinfo.json"
                            Condition="Exists('%(_AtlasModFolder.ModProjectDir)modinfo.json')">
        <StageDir>%(_AtlasModFolder.StageDir)</StageDir>
      </_AtlasModProjectFile>
    </ItemGroup>

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_AtlasModBuildFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(_AtlasModBuildFile->'%(StageDir)%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
          Condition="'@(_AtlasModBuildFile)' != ''" />
    <Copy SourceFiles="@(_AtlasModProjectFile)"
          DestinationFiles="@(_AtlasModProjectFile->'%(StageDir)%(RecursiveDir)%(Filename)%(Extension)')"
          Condition="'@(_AtlasModProjectFile)' != ''" />

    <ItemGroup>
      <_AtlasModReferencePath Include="@(_AtlasModDll->'%(FullPath)')" />
      <_AtlasModReferencePath Include="@(_AtlasModFolder->'%(StageFolder)')" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <MakeDir Directories="$(TargetDir)" Condition="'@(_AtlasModReferencePath)' != '' AND !Exists('$(TargetDir)')" />

    <WriteLinesToFile File="$(TargetDir)atlas-mods.generated.txt"
                       Lines="@(_AtlasModReferencePath)"
                       Overwrite="true"
                       Condition="'@(_AtlasModReferencePath)' != ''" />

    <Delete Files="$(TargetDir)atlas-mods.generated.txt"
            Condition="'@(_AtlasModReferencePath)' == '' AND Exists('$(TargetDir)atlas-mods.generated.txt')" />
  </Target>

  <!--
    Fail fast on xUnit v3 (issue found from a Discord install report, 2026-09-23): Atlas's
    execution engine is built on xUnit v2 extensibility (xunit.extensibility.execution
    2.9.x, referenced by Pixnop.Atlas.XUnit) and cannot discover or run scenarios through
    xUnit v3's runner. `dotnet new install xunit.v3.templates` then `dotnet new xunit3` is the
    separately installed xunit3 template (the SDK ships only `xunit`, the v2 one); the
    template itself defaults to net8.0, where restore fails first with NU1202 (Atlas's own
    dependencies are net10.0-only), so this target only ever runs on a net10.0 project. There,
    adding Pixnop.Atlas.XUnit to a v3-templated project restores clean (its xunit.v3.*
    packages and Atlas's transitive xunit v2 packages do not collide by version, so NuGet sees
    no conflict), and the build only fails once CoreCompile runs, with FactAttribute ambiguous
    between xunit.core (v2) and xunit.v3.core (v3):
    CS0433, a wall of compiler noise with no mention of xUnit versions at all. This target
    turns that into one clear error, before CoreCompile.

    @(ReferencePath) is populated by ResolveAssemblyReferences, after NuGet resolution; each
    item's NuGetPackageId metadata names the package an assembly came from, direct or
    transitive (a consumer never adds xunit.v3.core itself, it rides in with the xunit3
    template's own xunit.v3.mtp-v2 package). Matched by prefix, case-insensitively, against
    every xunit.v3.* package id (xunit.v3, xunit.v3.core, xunit.v3.assert, xunit.v3.common,
    xunit.v3.extensibility.core, xunit.v3.runner.*, xunit.v3.mtp-v2, and whatever else the
    family adds), not an exact list, since the v3 packages have shipped new members before.

    This repo's own projects and an ordinary xUnit v2 consumer carry no xunit.v3.* package,
    so the condition below never matches and the target costs one item transform per build,
    same cost note as WriteAtlasModManifest above.
  -->
  <Target Name="AtlasRejectXunitV3" AfterTargets="ResolveAssemblyReferences" BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
          Condition="'$(TargetFramework)' != ''">
    <ItemGroup>
      <_AtlasXunitV3Reference Include="@(ReferencePath)"
                              Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(ReferencePath.NuGetPackageId)').StartsWith('xunit.v3', System.StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase))" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <Error Condition="'@(_AtlasXunitV3Reference)' != ''"
           Code="ATLAS001"
           Text="Atlas runs on xUnit v2, not xUnit v3. Found in this project's reference graph: @(_AtlasXunitV3Reference->'%(NuGetPackageId)'->Distinct(), ', '). Remove the xunit.v3.* packages (and the xunit3 template's own global.json 'test' runner entry, if one was generated) and start the test project from `dotnet new xunit` instead (see https://github.com/Pixnop/Atlas/wiki/Troubleshooting)." />
  </Target>

</Project>
